y": "medium", "category": "bug", "title": "JSON.parse of decrypted backup codes has no error handling", "body": "JSON.parse(symmetricDecrypt(user.backupCodes, process.env.CALENDSO_ENCRYPTION_KEY)) will throw if the stored ciphertext is corrupted, the encryption key is rotated, or the data shape is unexpected. An uncaught throw here aborts the entire authorize() callback and surfaces as an InternalServerError, locking the user out. Wrap the decrypt + JSON.parse in try/catch and convert failures into a clean ErrorCode (e.g. MissingBackupCodes or InternalServerError) so authentication degrades gracefully."}, {"file": "apps/web/pages/api/auth/two-factor/totp/disable.ts", "line": 58, "severity": "medium", "category": "bug", "title": "JSON.parse of decrypted backup codes has no error handling", "body": "Same issue as in next-auth-options.ts: JSON.parse(symmetricDecrypt(...)) is unguarded. A corrupt/rotated backupCodes blob will crash the disable endpoint with an uncaught exception (500), preventing the user from disabling 2FA. Catch the error and return a typed error (e.g. MissingBackupCodes) instead."}, {"file": "packages/features/auth/lib/next-auth-options.ts", "line": 144, "severity": "medium", "category": "security", "title": "Backup code is not atomically consumed; concurrent logins can reuse a code", "body": "The backup code is looked up with backupCodes.indexOf(...) and then nulled out in a separate prisma.user.update. Between the read and the write, two concurrent signIn attempts (e.g. two browser tabs) can both observe the same code at the same index, both null it, and both be authenticated, allowing a single-use backup code to be used more than once. The same race exists in disable.ts (the indexOf succeeds but the consumed code is only later wiped by the unconditional backupCodes: null in the final update). Use a conditional update (e.g. updateMany where the code is still present, or store used codes with a unique marker) so consumption is atomic."}, {"file": "apps/web/components/auth/BackupCode.tsx", "line": 7, "severity": "low", "category": "bug", "title": "BackupCode component is exported as default function TwoFactor", "body": "The file apps/web/components/auth/BackupCode.tsx declares `export default function TwoFactor(...)`. Two other components in this PR are also named TwoFactor (apps/web/components/auth/TwoFactor.tsx and EnableTwoFactorModal imports it). The mismatch causes confusion in stack traces / DevTools and creates a real collision risk if anyone re-exports these modules together. Rename the function to BackupCode (or BackupCodeInput) to match the file name and the other new component."}, {"file": "apps/web/components/settings/EnableTwoFactorModal.tsx", "line": 95, "severity": "low", "category": "style", "title": "formatBackupCode referenced before it is defined", "body": "handleSetup uses formatBackupCode at line 95, but formatBackupCode is declared as a const at line 163, below the handler. This works only because handleSetup is invoked asynchronously after render. It is fragile (moving handleSetup to a hook or memoizing it would TDZ-crash) and makes the file hard to read. Move the formatBackupCode declaration above its first use."}, {"file": "apps/web/components/settings/EnableTwoFactorModal.tsx", "line": 64, "severity": "low", "category": "bug", "title": "backupCodes state is implicitly typed as never[]", "body": "`const [backupCodes, setBackupCodes] = useState([]);` infers never[], so .map(formatBackupCode) on body.backupCodes only compiles because body.backupCodes is any. There is no compile-time guarantee that the state actually contains strings, and the explicit type <string[]>([]) (or the inferred type from body.backupCodes) would catch a future API shape change."}, {"file": "packages/features/auth/lib/next-auth-options.ts", "line": 144, "severity": "low", "category": "data", "title": "Exhausted backup codes surface as IncorrectBackupCode, not MissingBackupCodes", "body": "After a user has consumed 